# Line endings. This repo is edited on Windows and run by CI on Linux, which
# is where the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings came from: Git for
# Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so every add rewrote line endings and
# said so. Pinning the working-tree ending here overrides autocrlf on both
# machines, so nothing is converted and nothing warns.

# LF everywhere, in the repo and in the working tree. Python, git bash and
# every editor in use read LF fine on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except the Windows launcher: cmd.exe is the one tool that still wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# The synthetic sheet is data, not source. `scripts/generate_synthetic_sheet.py`
# writes it through csv.writer, which emits CRLF on every platform because that
# is what a spreadsheet export looks like — the file mirrors the real sheet's
# format, dirtiness included. Left unconverted so the bytes on disk match the
# bytes in the index: convert it either way and a local generator run, or CI's,
# would leave `git status` dirty with a whole-file diff.
data/synthetic/*.csv -text

# Binary, so Git never treats a screenshot as text to convert or diff.
*.png binary
